'''tribalism''' (trī′bal-izm), ''n.'' [< ''tribal'' + ''-ism.''] The state of existing in separate tribes; tribal relation or feeling.

{{smaller block|No national life, much less civilisation, was possible under the system of Celtic ''tribalism'', as it existed at least till the time of the Tudors. {{right|''Edinburgh Rev.'', CLXIII. 443.}}}}

{{smaller block|The period of the Judges was one of entire ''tribalism'', with little national union and continuous relapses into idolatry. {{right|''The American'', XVII. 104.}}}}

'''tribally''' (trī′bal-i), ''adv.'' In a tribal manner; as or with reference to a tribe.

{{smaller block|It is probable that Professor Putnam is not justified in concluding that the people of the two sections were ''tribally'' identical. {{right|''Science'', XV. 383.}}}}

'''tribasic''' (trī-bā′sik), ''a.'' [< Gr. τρεῖς (τρι-), three, + βάσις, base, + ''-ic.''] In ''chem.'', having three hydrogen atoms replaceable by equivalents of a base: noting some acids.

